.TH MACH 1 "November 2, 2026" "Mach 0.15.0" "Mach Manual"
.SH NAME
mach \- Linux virtualization and command runner for macOS Apple Silicon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mach
.br
.B mach
[\fB\-m\fR \fImachine\fR] [\fB\-u\fR \fIuser\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIdir\fR]
\fIcommand\fR [\fIargs...\fR]
.br
.B mach
\fIsubcommand\fR [\fIargs...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mach
runs Linux virtual machines on Apple Silicon using Apple's
\fBVirtualization.framework\fR through Rust and Objective-C. First use may
download an image, create a disk, boot Linux, and wait for SSH. Performance
depends on the host and guest workload.

\fBmach\fR can be used in three distinct ways:

.TP
.B 1. Interactive Linux Shell
Running \fBmach\fR with no arguments opens an interactive login shell inside
the default Linux machine.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mach
root@debian:~# uname -a
Linux debian 6.1.0-53-cloud-arm64 #1 SMP aarch64
.fi
.RE

.TP
.B 2. Transparent Command Execution
Prefix a guest command with \fBmach\fR to execute it through SSH. Local
lifecycle verbs are reserved. Output and the remote command exit status are
returned to the terminal; SSH transport failures can return status 255.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mach uname -a
$ mach cat /etc/os-release
$ mach -m debian -u root apt update
.fi
.RE

.TP
.B 3. Machine & Subsystem Management
Basic lifecycle verbs are reserved by \fBmach\fR: start, stop, restart, shell,
create, delete, list (ls), and menu. Use \fBmachctl\fR for administration. In
particular, \fBmach docker\fR executes the guest Docker CLI; it does not
manage the host integration.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mach start debian
$ machctl top
$ machctl docker start
$ machctl k8s enable
.fi
.RE

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " " machine ", " \-\-machine " " machine
Specify the target Linux virtual machine (defaults to the configured default
machine, or the first running machine).
.TP
.BI \-u " " user ", " \-\-user " " user
Specify the guest username to run the command as (e.g., \fIroot\fR,
\fIdebian\fR, \fIubuntu\fR).
.TP
.BI \-w " " dir ", " \-\-workdir " " dir
Set the working directory inside the guest VM before executing the command.
Failure to enter an explicitly selected directory fails the command. By
default, a host working directory under /Users is used when it exists in the
guest; otherwise the guest home directory is used.
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
Print summary help and options.
.TP
.B \-V\fR, \fB\-\-version
Print the Mach version number.

.SH COMMAND ARGUMENTS
Use \fBmach -- command args...\fR to bypass local command routing for a guest
executable with a reserved name. Arguments are passed literally through SSH.
For guest shell expansion, invoke a shell explicitly, for example \fBmach sh
-c 'printf "%s\\n" "$HOME"'\fR. Machine selection uses \fB-m\fR; a leading
command argument is not interpreted as a machine name. Interactive terminals
request an SSH PTY; redirected input/output uses a non-PTY session.

.SH ENVIRONMENT & FILE SHARING
.TP
.B /Users Parity
The entire macOS \fB/Users\fR hierarchy is shared directly into the Linux
guest via Apple VirtioFS at \fB/Users\fR, providing matching host and guest
paths. Guest commands can access shared host files.
.TP
.B Dynamic Port Forwarding
Container ports exposed inside the guest VM are automatically bound on macOS
localhost (\fB127.0.0.1\fR) in user space without requiring root privileges.
.TP
.B Local DNS Routing
Machine and container domain integration uses the local \fB*.mach.local\fR
domain through the background responder and macOS resolver configuration.
Availability depends on service health and the target workload.
.TP
.B Rosetta 2 Translation
Intel \fBx86_64\fR binaries and Linux containers can use Apple Rosetta 2 for
Linux when available and configured (\fBdocker run --platform linux/amd64\fR).

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B Open default Linux shell
mach
.TP
.B Run a quick Linux command from macOS
mach uname -m
.TP
.B Execute as root in a specific machine
mach -m fedora -u root dnf install -y htop
.TP
.B Monitor resources in real-time
machctl top
.TP
.B Configure Docker CLI context
machctl docker start

